  "activity": {
    "id": "activity_vectors_projectile",
    "name": "Vectors and Projectile Motion Lab",
    "description": "An interactive AR lab exploring vector mathematics and two-dimensional projectile motion. Students begin by learning vector addition using the tip-to-tail method, then progress to decomposing vectors into horizontal and vertical components using trigonometry. Finally, students apply these concepts to projectile motion, observing how launch angle and speed affect trajectory, range, and maximum height, and understanding the independence of horizontal and vertical motions.",
    "workplace": "physics_vectors_projectile",
    "start": "action_vector_introduction",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "action_vector_introduction",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Introduction to Vectors",
          "description": "Vectors are quantities with both magnitude and direction. In this lab, we will explore how to represent vectors mathematically and combine them graphically. Watch the overview video and listen to the audio explanation to understand what vectors are and why they are essential in physics."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_vector_table",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_explanation_audio"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_vector_table",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_vector_addition_diagram",
              "poi": "vector_a_origin"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_vector_table",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_vector_addition_animation",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "action_vector_addition"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "action_vector_addition",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Vector Addition: Tip-to-Tail Method",
          "description": "Two vectors can be added by placing the tail of the second vector at the tip of the first vector. The resultant vector is drawn from the tail of the first vector to the tip of the second. Watch the animation to see this process in action, then observe how changing the vectors affects the resultant."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_vector_table",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_vector_addition_animation",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_decomposition_board",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_vector_components_diagram",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_vector_table",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "action_vector_decomposition"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "action_vector_decomposition",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Vector Decomposition into Components",
          "description": "A vector can be decomposed into horizontal (x) and vertical (y) components using right-triangle trigonometry. The magnitude of the vector and its direction angle determine the sizes of the components. Use the equations: Vx = V\u00b7cos(\u03b8) and Vy = V\u00b7sin(\u03b8). Watch the animation to see how a single vector is separated into its components."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_decomposition_board",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_decomposition_animation",
              "poi": "component_vectors_display"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_launcher",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_projectile_trajectory_video",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "place_decomposition_board",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "action_projectile_motion_intro"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "action_projectile_motion_intro",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Projectile Motion Overview",
          "description": "A projectile is an object launched into the air at an angle. Its initial velocity can be decomposed into horizontal and vertical components. The horizontal component remains constant (no horizontal force acts on the projectile), while the vertical component changes due to gravity. This independence of horizontal and vertical motions is a cornerstone of mechanics. Watch the overview video and the diagram showing independent motion components."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_launcher",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_projectile_trajectory_video",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_flight_domain",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_independence_of_motion_diagram",
              "poi": "trajectory_path"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_launcher",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_velocity_vector_animation",
              "poi": "velocity_vector_display"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "action_trajectory_analysis"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "action_trajectory_analysis",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Trajectory, Range, and Maximum Height",
          "description": "The trajectory of a projectile is parabolic, resulting from the combination of constant horizontal velocity and uniformly accelerated vertical motion. The range (horizontal distance traveled) and maximum height are determined by the initial speed and launch angle. At maximum height, the vertical velocity is zero. Observe the parabolic path and see how the velocity vector changes throughout the flight. Notice that at the apex (maximum height), the velocity vector points purely horizontally."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_flight_domain",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_trajectory_curve_animation",
              "poi": "trajectory_path"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_flight_domain",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_max_height_indicator",
              "poi": "apex_indicator"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_measurement_grid",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_launch_angle_comparison_video",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "place_flight_domain",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "action_launch_angle_effect"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "action_launch_angle_effect",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Effect of Launch Angle on Range and Height",
          "description": "The launch angle dramatically affects the trajectory, range, and maximum height of a projectile. A 45\u00b0 launch angle produces the maximum range for a given launch speed. Lower angles produce longer horizontal distances but lower maximum heights, while higher angles produce greater heights but shorter ranges. Watch the comparison video to see how different launch angles change the path of the projectile. Then examine the measurement grid to see the distances traveled."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_measurement_grid",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_launch_angle_comparison_video",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_measurement_grid",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_projectile_audio_guide",
              "poi": "range_readout"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_measurement_grid",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": []
      }
    ]
